---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../../lib/practice';
import testExample from '../../samples/test-example.ts.txt?raw';
import testFailure from '../../samples/test-failure.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const startSample = [
   `import { screenReader } from 'a11ied/test';`,
   '',
   `await using sr = await screenReader({ url: 'http://localhost:3000/checkout' });`,
   '',
   `await sr.next('heading');`,
   `await sr.expectSpoken('heading, Checkout, level 1');`,
].join('\n');
const assertSample = [
   `sr.checkpoint('opened');`,
   'sr.activate();',
   `sr.expectSpoken('dialog, Invite member', { since: 'opened' });`,
   `sr.expectSpoken(/error/iu, { not: true });`,
   `sr.expectCursorOn({ role: 'textbox', name: 'Email address' });`,
   `sr.expectSpokenInOrder(['dialog, Invite member', 'textbox, Email address'], { since: 'opened' });`,
].join('\n');
const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'example', label: 'A first test' },
   { id: 'start', label: 'Start and stop a reader' },
   { id: 'queue', label: 'Queued or awaited' },
   { id: 'methods', label: 'Move, type, and read' },
   { id: 'assert', label: 'Assertions' },
   { id: 'real', label: 'VoiceOver and NVDA' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Write screen reader tests"
   summary="<p>Drive a screen reader from a test and assert on what it announced. <code>a11ied/test</code> works with any test runner: Jest, Mocha, Playwright Test, Vitest, or a plain script.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npm install --save-dev a11ied</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>a11ied/test</code></dt>
         <dd>The screen reader API. Use it with any runner. This page covers it.</dd>
         <dt><code>a11ied/vitest</code></dt>
         <dd>The same API as a Vitest fixture, plus matchers. See <a href="/guides/vitest">test with Vitest</a>.</dd>
         <dt><code>a11ied/browser</code></dt>
         <dd>The same again for components mounted in Vitest browser mode.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Tests use the simulated screen reader unless you ask for another. A test that opens
         a URL needs Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium on the machine.
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code> adds one.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="example">A first test</h2>
      <p>
         This test runs against the docs' <a href="/guides/demo">practice page</a>, a demo
         page with a working copy and a broken copy. It opens a dialog and checks that the
         dialog announces its name and that focus moves into it.
      </p>
      <pre data-label="invite.test.ts"><code>{withPracticeURL(testExample, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <p>
         Run it against the broken copy and it fails with what the screen reader said:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{testFailure.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="start">Start and stop a reader</h2>
      <pre data-label="TypeScript"><code>{startSample}</code></pre>
      <p><code>screenReader()</code> takes these options.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>url</code> or <code>html</code></dt>
         <dd>The page to read. Inline <code>html</code> runs in jsdom and needs no browser, and page scripts do not run.</dd>
         <dt><code>sr</code></dt>
         <dd><code>'virtual'</code> by default, or <code>'voiceover'</code> or <code>'nvda'</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>mode</code></dt>
         <dd><code>'in-process'</code> by default: the reader lives inside the test, so tests in parallel workers do not share one. <code>'broker'</code> starts the session <code>a1 sr</code> uses, so you can inspect it from a terminal.</dd>
         <dt><code>timeoutMs</code></dt>
         <dd>A limit for each command.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <code>await using</code> stops the reader when the block ends, whether the test
         passed or threw. In a runner without <code>await using</code>, call
         <code>await sr.stop()</code> in the cleanup hook.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>sr.session</code> says what you got: <code>sr</code>, <code>mode</code>,
         <code>url</code>, and <code>engine</code>, which is <code>browser</code> or
         <code>jsdom</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="queue">Queued or awaited</h2>
      <p>There are two ways to call the same methods.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>screenReader()</code></dt>
         <dd>Every method returns a promise. Await each call.</dd>
         <dt><code>queuedScreenReader()</code></dt>
         <dd>A call queues a command and returns at once. Commands run in order. Await a call only when you need its value, as in <code>const item = await sr.read()</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         A queued reader keeps a test free of <code>await</code>. When the block ends,
         <code>await using</code> runs whatever is still queued, stops the reader, and
         rethrows the first failure. <code>sr.drain()</code> runs the queue without stopping.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="methods">Move, type, and read</h2>
      <p>
         The methods match the <a href="/guides/screen-reader"><code>a1 sr</code>
         commands</a>. A method that moves the cursor or sends input returns the
         announcement.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt><code>next(kind?)</code>, <code>previous(kind?)</code></dt>
         <dd>Move one element, or jump by kind: <code>'heading'</code>, <code>'link'</code>, <code>'button'</code>, and the rest. Options: <code>level</code>, <code>times</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>goTo(&#123; role, name &#125;)</code>, <code>find(text)</code></dt>
         <dd>Move forward to a matching element. Both throw when nothing matches.</dd>
         <dt><code>top()</code>, <code>bottom()</code></dt>
         <dd>Move to the first or last element.</dd>
         <dt><code>activate()</code>, <code>press(...chords)</code>, <code>type(text)</code>, <code>escape()</code></dt>
         <dd>Use a control. <code>press('Tab', 'Enter')</code> sends two chords in order.</dd>
         <dt><code>read()</code></dt>
         <dd>The element under the cursor: <code>role</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>states</code>, <code>level</code>, <code>phrase</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>elements(kind)</code>, <code>readAll()</code>, <code>walk()</code></dt>
         <dd>Every element of one kind, everything from the cursor on, and everything from the top.</dd>
         <dt><code>checkpoint(label)</code>, <code>transcript(&#123; since &#125;)</code></dt>
         <dd>Mark a point, and read what was announced since.</dd>
         <dt><code>wait(&#123; for &#125;)</code></dt>
         <dd>Wait for an announcement that has not happened yet. Throws after 5000 ms.</dd>
         <dt><code>open(url)</code></dt>
         <dd>Load another page in the same reader.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         <code>title()</code>, <code>table(move)</code>, <code>interact()</code>,
         <code>stopInteracting()</code>, <code>perform(command)</code>,
         <code>screenshot(path)</code>, and <code>focus()</code> complete the set. See the
         <a href="/reference/api#test">API reference</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="assert">Assertions</h2>
      <pre data-label="TypeScript"><code>{assertSample}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs defs--wide">
         <dt><code>expectSpoken(match, options?)</code></dt>
         <dd>An announcement contains the text, ignoring case, or matches the <code>RegExp</code>. <code>since</code> limits it to what came after a checkpoint. <code>not</code> passes when nothing matches.</dd>
         <dt><code>expectCursorOn(&#123; role, name &#125;)</code></dt>
         <dd>The element under the cursor has that role, that name, or both.</dd>
         <dt><code>expectSpokenInOrder([a, b, c])</code></dt>
         <dd>Each match comes after the one before it. Other announcements may come between.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         An assertion waits. It checks again every 100 ms until it passes or 5000 ms have
         gone by, so an announcement that arrives a moment after the key press still counts.
         Change the limit with <code>timeoutMs</code>. An assertion with <code>not</code>
         checks once.
      </p>
      <p>
         A failure throws <code>ScreenReaderAssertionError</code>. Its message says what was
         expected and what the screen reader said. It also has <code>expected</code>,
         <code>phrases</code>, and <code>exitCode: 4</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="real">VoiceOver and NVDA</h2>
      <p>
         <code>screenReader(&#123; sr: 'voiceover', url &#125;)</code> opens the URL in a browser
         and starts VoiceOver, as <code>a1 sr start</code> does. The machine needs
         <code>a1 setup</code> once.
      </p>
      <p>
         A real screen reader takes over the screen and reads whatever is in front. Run
         these tests one at a time, never in parallel workers. Use
         <code>mode: 'broker'</code>, so that a session left behind by a crashed test can be
         stopped with <code>a1 sr stop</code>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
